---
/* The shell for /about/ime/.

   IT WRAPS Base.astro RATHER THAN REPLACING IT, and it is not a second layout
   mechanism. docs/content-architecture.md §5 puts the About page in MDX so its
   content stays markdown; what MDX cannot carry is a scoped <style> block, and
   that is very nearly the entire contents of this file.

   IT USED TO CARRY THE HERO TOO — a hand-copied duplicate of index.astro's
   Masthead, markup and ~140 lines of restated CSS, with a note attached saying
   the two were one arrangement and that if one number moved both had to move.
   That note also recorded why a shared component had been refused, and named the
   condition that would overturn it: "worth revisiting if a third page ever opens
   this way". ADR-0011 is that revisit. The Masthead is Base.astro's now, this
   page passes `About` as its eyebrow, and the duplicate is gone.

   WHAT IS LEFT is the markdown styling below, which genuinely is this page's
   own: it is the only route on the site whose body is authored prose. */
import Base from './Base.astro';

/* THE FRONTMATTER ARRIVES UNDER `frontmatter`, NOT AS TOP-LEVEL PROPS, and the
   difference fails silently. A layout used by an .mdx page gets one object:
   `Astro.props.frontmatter`. Destructuring `title` off `Astro.props` directly —
   which is what a layout used by an .astro page would do — yields undefined,
   Base.astro renders `<title>undefined</title>`... except it does not even do
   that: it renders an EMPTY <title>, the page keeps its meta description and its
   canonical, and it looks completely normal.

   Caught by `document-title` in lighthouserc.cjs, which is exactly why that list
   names ten audits rather than leaning on the category score. Nothing in the
   build, in `astro check`, or on the rendered page said a word. */
interface Props {
  frontmatter: {
    title: string;
    description: string;
  };
}

/* The layout adds no metadata of its own — the Social Card, the canonical, and
   the robots tag are all Base.astro's, and a second place deciding any of them
   is how two mechanisms start drifting.

   `About` WHERE `/` SAYS `Selected work`. Same type role, same job: it names
   what the screen below it is. It is the one thing that differs between this
   page's Masthead and any other's. */
const { title, description } = Astro.props.frontmatter;
---

<Base title={title} description={description} eyebrow="About">
  {/* THE PORTRAIT HERE CARRIES NO ABOUT BAND, and Masthead.astro decides that
      rather than this file: it compares `Astro.url.pathname` against ABOUT_PATH,
      because a band here would be a control leading to the document it is
      already in. Passing it as a prop would make suppression every future
      page's job to remember. */}
  <div class="prose" data-anim="scroll">
    <slot />
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* ONE DATUM EDGE, and one column AT THE FULL SHELL. `/projects/<slug>/`
     argues this at length: everything on the page starts on the same left edge.
     That page's body was widened from --body-col to the shell, and this one
     follows it rather than keeping a second, narrower answer — two pages of
     prose at two different measures is the drift, not the consistency.

     The cap and the gutter are base.css's, on the rule that names every direct
     child of <body>, so nothing here needs a max-width of its own. */
  .prose {
    margin-top: var(--rhythm);
  }

  /* ---- markdown, styled through :global() ---------------------------------
     Astro scopes styles by attribute, and nothing inside <slot /> carries this
     component's attribute — the MDX renders in the page's scope, not the
     layout's. So every rule reaching the content is :global(), confined by the
     .prose ancestor. This is the same mechanism `/projects/<slug>/` uses for
     the <Picture> elements inside its gallery links, and the same one
     Meta.astro uses to reach the two ancestors it does not own.

     NO NEW TYPE ROLE AND NO NEW SIZE. h2 takes --t-h3, which is the Plate
     title's size on `/`; everything else is the serif body default or one of
     the two mono classes. Six styles, unchanged.

     THE h2 IS ALSO NOW CORRECT RATHER THAN CONVENIENT. tokens.css's rule is
     that h1 is the site's name and h2 is a page's own title; the Masthead
     carries the h1 on every route, so this page's headings start at h2 the way
     a Project's and /404's do. Nothing about the size changed. */
  .prose :global(h2) {
    margin: var(--rhythm) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--t-h3);
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  .prose :global(h2:first-child) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  /* NO COLOUR. This was --muted for one draft, which put the practice prose —
     the primary content of the page — in the secondary colour while the
     experience list beside it sat in --ink, so the eye went to the dated rows
     rather than to the writing. `/projects/<slug>/` sets the pattern: the lede
     is --muted and the body prose is the inherited --ink. There is no lede
     here, so there is nothing to demote. */
  .prose :global(p) {
    margin: var(--s3) 0 0;
  }

  /* The dated lists: experience and education. A rule per row rather than a
     bullet, because the site has no bullet and adding one would be a mark it
     does not otherwise use. The rule is --rule, the same hairline the fact
     block on a Project page separates itself with. */
  .prose :global(ul) {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--s3) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .prose :global(li) {
    padding: var(--s2) 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .prose :global(li:last-child) {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  /* The year sits above its line in mono, the line reads in serif. Authored in
     the MDX as <span class="t-spec">, so the markdown stays markdown and the
     type role is the site's own rather than a fourth thing invented here. */
  .prose :global(li .t-spec) {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: var(--s1);
  }

  /* Capabilities. The same <dl> shape as the fact block on a Project page,
     because it is the same object: a short label over a value, several across.
     Not a copy of those rules — that page's are scoped to it — but the same
     tokens, so the two read as one treatment. */
  .prose :global(dl) {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s3);
    margin: var(--s3) 0 0;
    padding: var(--s3) 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  @media (min-aspect-ratio: 1 / 1) {
    .prose :global(dl) {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: var(--s3) var(--s5);
    }
  }

  /* --ink, INHERITED AND THEREFORE NOT DECLARED. The values under Capabilities
     read --muted for one draft, on the argument that a <dt> label should lead
     its <dd>. It is backwards: the label is the furniture and the tool list IS
     the content — it is what a visitor scans this block for. The <dt>s keep
     .t-label's --muted, so the pair still reads as label over value.

     Same correction as the lede in the Masthead, made for the same reason: the
     secondary colour belongs on the things around the content, not on the
     content. */
  .prose :global(dd) {
    margin: var(--s1) 0 0;
  }

  /* The location line, and the last thing on the page. Contact is deliberately
     NOT repeated here: Footer.astro is on every page and carries the address
     and the LinkedIn link, and docs/content-architecture.md §1 is explicit that
     contact is not a route. */
  .prose :global(.location) {
    margin-top: var(--rhythm);
    padding-top: var(--s3);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
</style>
